gaGF(){return"Return vs Risk"},
gaGG(){return"Return vs Risk \u2014 P/L% \xf7 Beta\n\nHigher = better return for the volatility taken on.\nA stock up 30% with beta 0.8 scores better than one up 30% with beta 1.5.\n\nStocks with no beta data, or negative beta (inverse ETFs), sort to the bottom."},
gaGH(){return"Total Return"},
gaGI(){return'Total Return \u2014 (Unrealized P/L + Dividends on the shares you still hold) \xf7 Cost of those shares \xd7 100\n\nTrue all-in return on your current position, including both price appreciation and income.\nDividends earned by shares you have since sold belong to their realised P/L, not to this score \u2014 the amount beside each row is everything received.\nStocks with no investment recorded sort to the bottom, and so does a stock with a dividend whose exchange rate has not arrived yet (it shows "\u2014").'},
aGJ(a){return a+" total"},
gaGK(){return"<90 days held"},
gaGL(){return"Value Score"},
gaGM(){return"Value Score \u2014 100 \xf7 P/E ratio\n\nLower P/E = higher score = potentially cheaper relative to earnings.\nA P/E of 10 scores 10.0; a P/E of 50 scores 2.0.\n\nStocks with no P/E data (ETFs, no-earnings companies) sort to the bottom."},
aGN(a){return"  \xb7  "+a+"% yield"},
ga7o(){return"Read your profile"},
gaGY(){return"Real"},
aH1(a){return"Splits your total profit/loss into money you have LOCKED IN and money still FLOATING.\n\n\u2022 Locked In (Realized) \u2014 P/L on shares you have already sold (plus dividends attributed to them). This is booked; market moves no longer change it.\n\u2022 Floating (Unrealized) \u2014 P/L on shares you still hold. It moves with the market until you sell.\n\nBoth are in "+a+": each holding's result is measured in its own currency and converted at today's rate.\n\nThis Total P/L is not the Portfolio Summary's. It also counts fully sold holdings and the dividends on sold shares, and it leaves out how exchange rates have moved since you bought \u2014 so the two can differ.\n\nDividends on shares you still hold are not counted here (Real vs Paper counts them)."},
gaGZ(){return'This card uses the "Return on cost" lens.\n\nReturn on cost = your profit \xf7 the cost of everything it was earned on: the shares you still hold plus the shares you have sold. It answers: "how much has my money made on what I put at risk?"\n\nThe "Cost" row is that cost. P/L, Cost and Value are converted at today\'s exchange rates.\n\nThe "Do I Beat the Market?" card shows no return percentage. Its "You made" is your profit in money: what you hold today, plus the cash you got back from sells and dividends, minus everything you put in \u2014 with each trade at its own date\'s exchange rate where one exists, so it can differ from the P/L here.\n\nFor a yearly rate that accounts for when you invested, see the Personal IRR card.'},
gaH_(){return"Return on cost \u2014 profit \xf7 the cost of everything it was earned on. Includes sold positions and the dividends on shares you have sold (not those on shares you still hold), so it can differ from the Portfolio tab (which shows open holdings only)."},
gaH0(){return"Return on cost \u2014 profit \xf7 the cost of everything it was earned on. Start a Paper Portfolio to compare a risk-free test run against your real money side by side."},
gaH2(){return"Noted rounds did worse."},
